NIC (Real host NIC)






Speculative data Packet header structure	
[Default header][SRP_TYPE][TTW][SRC][PID][PORT]
	 -[Default header]: header_info_width, unmodified header from the baseline RTL
	 -[SRP_TYPE]: 2'b00 
	 -[TTW]: 8bits time to wait
	 -[SRC]: reply address
	 -[PID]: variable bits, identifier for external checker
	 -[PORT]: port_idx_width, identifier for external checker

Reservation request packet structure
[Default header][SRP_TYPE][RESERVATION_SIZE][SRC][PID][PORT]
	 -[SRP_TYPE]: 2'b00
	 -[RESERVATION_SIZE]: log2(max_flow_size)
	 -[SRC]: reply address

Grant request packet structure
[Default header][SRP_TYPE][FILLER][TIMESTAMP]
	 -[SRP_TYPE]: 2'b00
	 -[FILLER]: 'b0
	 -[TIMESTAMP]:32 bits

Ack request packet structure
[Default header][SRP_TYPE][FILLER]
	 -[SRP_TYPE]: 2'b01
	 -[FILLER]: 'b0


(1) Speculative timer overflow
Because the speuclative VC is inherently dynamic, timer overflow is more manageable than other situations. Hypothetically, n speculative packets [P1 to Pn] with length [psize] arrives in rapid succession right before timer overflow [P1_timestamp = tmax-(n-1)*psize-1 and Pn_timestamp = tmax-1]. For the HoL packet [P1] overflow detection is trivial as there is a timer check every cycle. After TTW cycles P1 is dropped and the worst case value for the timer is exactly tmax-(n-1)*psize-1+TTW-tmax simplifies to TTW-(n-1)*psize-1 [-tmax is due to overflow]. It takes psize cycles to drop P1, now timer =  TTW-(n-2)*psize-1. P2 is up for time check and if (P2_timestamp > timer) then overflow is again detectable. Substitute values and we get tmax > TTW.  Assume TTW on all packets are the the same or lower, then  the previous relations can be used to show that overflow will be detected on all n packets.  
Also TTW>psize, or tmax>ttw+psize. 



(2) Flow buffer control FSM
States
	EMPTY: ready for new flow
	RES_READY: ready to inject reservaiton packet
	SPEC_READY: ready to inject speculative packet
	SPEC_STOP: grant or nack received, finish transmittingthe current packet
	NORMAL: ready for non-spec transmission
Transitions
	flow_valid: EMPTY->RES_READY
	res_sent: RES_READY->SPEC_READY;
	grant_received: SPEC_READY->SPEC_STOP
	nack_received: SPEC_READY->SPEC_STOP
	curr_time==grant_time: SPEC_STOP->NORMAL
	packet_count==0: NORMAL->EMPTY
Non-transition events
	arb_grant: send a flit
	ack_received: update spec meta data
